    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/roger-ii-of-sicily"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/roger-ii-of-sicily.jpg" alt="Roger II of Sicily" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/roger-ii-of-sicily">Roger II of Sicily</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Martorana_Palermo_msu2017-0166.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Matthias Süßen, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> emerged from the break-up of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, created by the Norman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-ii-of-sicily">Roger II</a> in 1130 and later ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> emperors. In 1265 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-clement-iv">Pope Clement IV</a> invested <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-anjou">Charles of Anjou</a>, brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ix-of-france">Louis IX of France</a>, with the kingdom; in February 1266 Charles defeated and killed King Manfred at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-benevento">Battle of Benevento</a> and in 1268 defeated the last Hohenstaufen heir, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conradin">Conradin</a>, at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tagliacozzo">Battle of Tagliacozzo</a>, beheading him in Naples. Charles shifted the centre of government from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a> to Naples, where he built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castel-nuovo">Castel Nuovo</a>. Heavy taxation and the dominance of French officials provoked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicilian-vespers">Sicilian Vespers</a>, the uprising that broke out in Palermo on Easter Monday, 30 March 1282, and massacred the French on the island. The Sicilians offered their crown to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-iii-of-aragon">Peter III of Aragon</a>, husband of Manfred's daughter Constance, and Charles was left with the mainland only. The War of the Sicilian Vespers lasted twenty years: Charles's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-of-naples">Charles II</a>, captured at sea by the Aragonese admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-of-lauria">Roger of Lauria</a> in 1284, became king on his father's death in 1285 while still a prisoner, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-caltabellotta">Peace of Caltabellotta</a> in 1302 confirmed the division. The Angevin kings kept the title 'King of Sicily', but their mainland realm was ruled from Naples and became known by its capital's name.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/naples"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/naples.jpg" alt="Naples" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Napoli_-_Maschio_Angioino_-_202209302342_3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Richard Nevell, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The kingdom was overwhelmingly agrarian, and its society was dominated by a powerful feudal baronage and a numerous clergy. Grain from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a>, wine, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a> and wool were its main products; the great seasonal migration of sheep between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abruzzo">Abruzzi</a> mountains and the Apulian plain was organised by a royal customs office, the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dogana-della-mena-delle-pecore-di-puglia">Dogana della mena delle pecore</a></em>, founded under Alfonso V in 1447. Much of the long-distance trade was in the hands of Genoese, Florentine and Venetian merchants and bankers, who lent money to the crown and exported raw materials, while local manufacture remained modest. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> itself grew enormously, drawing in nobles, lawyers, clergy and the rural poor; by around 1600 it probably had well over 250,000 inhabitants, making it one of the largest cities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, until the plague of 1656 killed around half its population. The provinces, by contrast, were poor, burdened by feudal dues and troubled by banditry. The church was immensely rich, with hundreds of religious houses in the capital, and popular devotion centred on saints such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/januarius">San Gennaro</a>, whose blood is believed to liquefy. Cultural life flourished at court and in the capital, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giotto">Giotto</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrarch">Petrarch</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-king-of-naples">Robert the Wise</a> to the humanists of Alfonso V, the painting of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-caravaggio">Caravaggio</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luca-giordano">Luca Giordano</a>, and the Neapolitan school of opera in the eighteenth century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cerignola"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-cerignola.jpg" alt="Battle of Cerignola" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cerignola">Battle of Cerignola</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Elgrancapitantrasbatalladeceri%C3%B1ola.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Federico de Madrazo y Kuntz, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Angevin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> spent much of the fourteenth century trying to recover <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> through repeated expeditions that failed, and its kings led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guelphs-and-ghibellines">Guelf</a> cause in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-king-of-naples">Robert the Wise</a> was lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> from 1318 and the chief opponent of the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-vii-of-germany">Henry VII</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ladislaus-of-naples">Ladislaus of Naples</a> (r. 1386–1414) built one of the strongest armies in Italy with condottieri, occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1408 and threatened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> before his sudden death. The Ottoman capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otranto">Otranto</a> in 1480, where many inhabitants were killed, exposed the vulnerability of the long coastline until the town was recovered in 1481. Naples then became a prize in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-wars">Italian Wars</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-viii-of-france">Charles VIII of France</a> entered the city in February 1495 almost unopposed but soon withdrew; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xii-of-france">Louis XII</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-ii-of-aragon">Ferdinand II of Aragon</a> agreed in 1500 to partition the kingdom, then fell out, and the Spanish commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gonzalo-fernandez-de-cordoba">Gonzalo Fernández de Córdoba</a>, the 'Great Captain', won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cerignola">Battle of Cerignola</a>, one of the first battles decided largely by handguns, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-garigliano-1503">Battle of the Garigliano</a>, both in 1503. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> the kingdom supplied soldiers, money and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> wars, including ships at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lepanto">Battle of Lepanto</a> in 1571, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroys</a> built chains of coastal watchtowers against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbary-coast">Barbary</a> corsairs. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iii-of-spain">Charles of Bourbon</a> won the kingdom at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bitonto">Battle of Bitonto</a> in 1734, but the army of his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-of-the-two-sicilies">Ferdinand IV</a> collapsed against the French in 1798.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii-elector-of-brandenburg"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frederick-ii-elector-of-brandenburg.jpg" alt="Frederick II, Elector of Brandenburg" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii-elector-of-brandenburg">Frederick II, Elector of Brandenburg</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arolsen_Klebeband_01_203_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The kingdom inherited from the Normans and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii-elector-of-brandenburg">Frederick II</a> a centralised royal administration, with justiciars over the provinces, a royal treasury and a high court, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capetian-house-of-anjou">Angevins</a> granted extensive lands and jurisdiction to French and local barons, and royal authority depended heavily on these lords. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-king-of-naples">Robert the Wise</a> (r. 1309–1343), praised as a learned 'new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>', ruled through an able bureaucracy and close ties with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a>. The long reign of his granddaughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joanna-duchess-of-brabant">Joanna</a> I (1343–1382), marked by the murder of her husband <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-duke-of-calabria">Andrew of Hungary</a> in 1345, a Hungarian invasion and the Western Schism, ended with her deposition and death at the hands of her cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iii-of-naples">Charles of Durazzo</a>. Alfonso V of Aragon, who conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> in 1442, reorganised finances and justice, creating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sacro-regio-consiglio">Sacro Regio Consiglio</a> as supreme tribunal; his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-of-naples">Ferrante</a> crushed the Conspiracy of the Barons of 1485–1486. Under Spanish rule from 1504 a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a> governed with the Collateral Council, while a parliament of barons and towns voted taxes until it ceased to meet in the 1640s. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-alvarez-de-toledo-marquis-of-villafranca">Pedro de Toledo</a>, viceroy from 1532 to 1553, strengthened royal justice and remodelled the capital, laying out the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/via-toledo">Via Toledo</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbons</a> from 1734, with ministers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernardo-tanucci">Bernardo Tanucci</a>, tried to curb church privileges and baronial power, but feudalism was abolished only under French rule in 1806.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/castel-nuovo"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/castel-nuovo.jpg" alt="Castel Nuovo" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/castel-nuovo">Castel Nuovo</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Municip.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Little john at Italian Wikipedia, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For over five centuries <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> was the largest state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and its capital one of the great cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-king-of-naples">Robert the Wise</a> the Angevin court attracted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giotto">Giotto</a>, who worked there around 1328–1333, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrarch">Petrarch</a>, who was examined by the king in 1341 before his coronation as poet laureate in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, while the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-boccaccio">Giovanni Boccaccio</a> spent formative years in the city. Alfonso V made Naples a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> humanism, patronising scholars such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorenzo-valla">Lorenzo Valla</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-beccadelli">Antonio Beccadelli</a>, whose circle grew into the academy later named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-pontano">Giovanni Pontano</a>, and the triumphal arch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castel-nuovo">Castel Nuovo</a> is among the earliest Renaissance monuments of southern Italy. As a Spanish dominion the kingdom was a major source of revenue, men and ships for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> and a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> art, music and learning. The philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giambattista-vico">Giambattista Vico</a> published his <em>New Science</em> in Naples in 1725, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pietro-giannone">Pietro Giannone</a>'s <em>Civil History of the Kingdom of Naples</em> attacked the temporal power of the church, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-genovesi">Antonio Genovesi</a> held one of Europe's first chairs of political economy from 1754. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iii-of-spain">Charles of Bourbon</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teatro-di-san-carlo">Teatro di San Carlo</a> in 1737, began the palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-palace-of-caserta">Caserta</a> and sponsored the excavations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herculaneum">Herculaneum</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompeii">Pompeii</a>, which shaped European <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neoclassicism">Neoclassicism</a>. The kingdom's legacy of feudal landholding, weak provincial towns and fiscal pressure also lies behind the long-debated 'Southern Question' of united Italy.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tolentino"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-tolentino.jpg" alt="Battle of Tolentino" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tolentino">Battle of Tolentino</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Tolentino.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The end of the kingdom came through revolution, French conquest and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> restoration. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-of-the-two-sicilies">Ferdinand IV</a>, who succeeded in 1759 when his father Charles left to become king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-carolina-of-austria">Maria Carolina of Austria</a>, sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marie-antoinette">Marie Antoinette</a>, turned against reformers after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>. In 1798 Ferdinand attacked the French in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, but his army was routed; the royal family fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horatio-nelson">Nelson</a>'s ships, and in January 1799 French troops and Neapolitan patriots proclaimed the Parthenopean Republic. Within months the peasant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/army-of-the-holy-faith">Army of the Holy Faith</a> (<em>Sanfedisti</em>) led by Cardinal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fabrizio-ruffo">Fabrizio Ruffo</a> retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, and the restored king executed many of the republic's leaders, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleonora-fonseca-pimentel">Eleonora Fonseca Pimentel</a>. In 1806 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s armies drove Ferdinand back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, where he reigned under British protection, while the mainland became a French satellite under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-bonaparte">Joseph Bonaparte</a>, who abolished feudalism, and from 1808 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joachim-murat">Joachim Murat</a>, who modernised the administration and law. After Murat's defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tolentino">Battle of Tolentino</a> in 1815, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> restored Ferdinand. By a law of 8 December 1816 he merged his two realms into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-two-sicilies">Kingdom of the Two Sicilies</a>, taking the title Ferdinand I, and the separate Kingdom of Naples ceased to exist. The new state lasted until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Garibaldi</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/expedition-of-the-thousand">Expedition of the Thousand</a> in 1860 and its annexation to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a> in 1861.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> was for five centuries the largest state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, ruled from one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s greatest cities by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capetian-house-of-anjou">Angevins</a>, Aragonese, Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroys</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbons</a>; a centre of humanism, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> culture and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>, it also left the feudal and fiscal legacy that shaped the later history of southern Italy.</em></p>

